---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — Fitwise.stream"
  description="How Fitwise sources dimensions and calculates fit results."
  noindex
>
  <section class="wrapper section">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p>
      Fitwise uses millimetres as its internal calculation unit. Inputs may be typed as mm, cm, m,
      inches or feet and inches; display units can be switched independently. Sourced product
      measurements, geometry derived from explicit formulas and user-selected assumptions are kept
      distinct.
    </p>
    <h2>Fit states</h2>
    <p>
      In dimension tables, <strong>physical margin</strong> is available space minus hard footprint.
      <strong>Target margin</strong> is available space minus the selected or sourced target. A
      positive physical margin does not mean a clearance target is met.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Fits:</strong> every hard dimension and every selected clearance target is met.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tight:</strong> physical footprints fit, but at least one selected target is short.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Does not fit:</strong> at least one physical or enabled access dimension exceeds the
        available space.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Generic item and access review</h2>
    <p>
      The universal calculator compares a rectangular item's width, depth and height with a room's
      inside dimensions, testing the two axis-aligned floor orientations. Optional door and corridor
      checks use an upright narrow face and item height. The model does not simulate tilting,
      diagonal passage, turns, stairs, handles, packaging bulges, thresholds, sloping ceilings or
      irregular room geometry. Measure the narrowest clear point along the full route.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quantity output is a maximum single-layer rectangular grid estimate with the entered gap. It
      does not model stacking, load limits, access lanes, supports or obstacles. Mattress and
      workspace planners have their own explicit orientation and clearance assumptions.
    </p>
    <h2>What a result does not mean</h2>
    <p>
      A calculation is not a delivery guarantee, building-code determination, accessibility check,
      safety assessment or ergonomic recommendation. Manufacturer dimensions can vary by model and
      revision; verify the exact item's external dimensions and the site's clear openings before
      buying, moving or installing it. Any clearance target not attributed to a specific source is a
      user-selected planning preference, not a universal minimum.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/methodology/reference-conventions/">
        See public measurement and reference conventions
      </a>
      and <a href="/about/#data-sources">the source registry</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

